<main class="layout-container layout-stack-main">

  <header class="surface-bento page-hero-bento">
    <h1 class="text-page-title">Mold, Moisture &amp; PNW Attic Rain</h1>
    <p class="text-body page-hero-lead">
      Understanding indoor humidity in King and Snohomish counties: why mold is a ventilation issue rather than a cleaning defect, demystifying black-colored surface mildew, fixing winter attic rain, and protecting crawl spaces.
    </p>
  </header>

  <div class="section-divider"></div>

  <section class="content-section-centered">
    <article class="surface-bento calc-strategy-card">
      <h2 class="text-widget-title">Why Moisture Control Is the True Key to Mold Control</h2>
      <p class="text-body">
        Mold spores exist naturally everywhere in the Pacific Northwest—outdoor air carries them year-round. The EPA explicitly confirms that moisture control is the key to mold control. Simply wiping down mold with bleach will not resolve the issue if you do not address the underlying ventilation or humidity source. Conversely, when you restore ventilation and dry out the space, mold stops growing and dies off naturally. In addition, 99 percent of dark spots found on window sills or bath tile in Western Washington are harmless surface mildew caused by trapped humidity, not dangerous toxic black mold.
      </p>
    </article>
  </section>

  <div class="section-divider"></div>

  <section class="content-section-centered">
    <h2 class="text-section-title">The "Black Mold" Myth vs. Common Surface Mildew</h2>
    <p class="text-body section-body-prose">
      Dark mold spots around window sills or bathroom grout lines frequently cause unnecessary panic during home inspections. Understanding the biological difference protects both health and transaction leverage.
    </p>

    <div class="section-grid-static grid-cols-2">
      
      <article class="surface-bento calc-strategy-card">
        <header class="text-center">
          <div class="text-overline text-accent-alpine">High Prevalence / Non-Toxic</div>
          <h3 class="text-widget-title">Common Surface Mildew</h3>
        </header>
        <p class="text-body">
          Almost all dark spots in PNW homes are standard surface mildew (Cladosporium or Alternaria). It develops on cold window glass condensation or shower caulking when bathroom exhaust fan usage is insufficient. It is easily wiped away and permanently prevented by running bath fans for 20 minutes after bathing.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="surface-bento calc-strategy-card">
        <header class="text-center">
          <div class="text-overline text-accent-alpine">Rare / Active Leak Required</div>
          <h3 class="text-widget-title">Toxic Black Mold (Stachybotrys)</h3>
        </header>
        <p class="text-body">
          True toxic black mold (Stachybotrys chartarum) requires continuously saturated, paper-faced drywall or wood that has been soaked by active, ongoing plumbing or roof leaks for multiple weeks. It is slimy, dark greenish-black, and exceptionally rare in properly maintained and ventilated homes.
        </p>
      </article>

    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="section-divider"></div>

  <section class="content-section-centered">
    <h2 class="text-section-title">Demystifying PNW "Attic Rain"</h2>
    <p class="text-body section-body-prose">
      During cold Snohomish and King County winters, homeowners often inspect their attics and find damp roof plywood or water dripping onto insulation. In over 90 percent of cases, this isn't a roof leak—it is a classic regional condensation cycle known as Attic Rain.
    </p>

    <div class="surface-bento calc-strategy-card" style="padding: 1.25rem;">
      <div style="display: flex; align-items: flex-start; gap: 1rem; padding: 1rem; background: var(--color-surface-gray); border-radius: 12px; margin-bottom: 0.75rem;">
        <div style="width: 100px; flex-shrink: 0;">
          <span class="text-overline text-accent-alpine">Phase 01</span>
        </div>
        <div>
          <strong class="text-primary-charcoal" style="display: block; margin-bottom: 0.25rem;">Vapor Ascent</strong>
          <span class="text-body">Warm, moisture-laden air from cooking and unvented showers rises into cold attic cavities through unsealed bypasses or disconnected flexible ductwork.</span>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div style="display: flex; align-items: flex-start; gap: 1rem; padding: 1rem;">
        <div style="width: 100px; flex-shrink: 0;">
          <span class="text-overline text-accent-alpine">Phase 02</span>
        </div>
        <div>
          <strong class="text-primary-charcoal" style="display: block; margin-bottom: 0.25rem;">Nocturnal Freezing</strong>
          <span class="text-body">Warm vapor strikes freezing roof sheathing on cold winter nights, forming heavy frost and ice layers on the underside of plywood decking.</span>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div style="display: flex; align-items: flex-start; gap: 1rem; padding: 1rem; background: var(--color-surface-gray); border-radius: 12px;">
        <div style="width: 100px; flex-shrink: 0;">
          <span class="text-overline text-accent-alpine">Phase 03</span>
        </div>
        <div>
          <strong class="text-primary-charcoal" style="display: block; margin-bottom: 0.25rem;">Morning Thaw &amp; Staining</strong>
          <span class="text-body">Morning solar radiation rapidly warms the roof deck, melting frost instantly and causing water to rain down into insulation and ceiling drywall.</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="section-divider"></div>

  <section class="content-section-centered">
    <h2 class="text-section-title">Problem Area, Root Cause &amp; Permanent Fix</h2>
    <p class="text-body section-body-prose">
      Solving moisture issues in Western Washington requires eliminating the source and establishing proper airflow paths rather than applying temporary surface treatments.
    </p>

    <div class="section-grid-static grid-cols-1">
      
      <article class="surface-bento calc-strategy-card">
        <header class="text-center">
          <div class="text-overline text-accent-alpine">Attic Cavity</div>
          <h3 class="text-widget-title">Roof Sheathing Stains &amp; Frost ("Attic Rain")</h3>
        </header>

        <div class="section-grid-static grid-cols-2">
          <div class="calc-field-group">
            <span class="calc-field-label">Primary Root Cause</span>
            <p class="text-body">Bathroom exhaust fans terminating directly inside the attic space, disconnected flex ducting, or soffit intake vents blocked by blown-in insulation.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="calc-field-group">
            <span class="calc-field-label">Permanent Mechanical Fix</span>
            <p class="text-body">Reroute exhaust ductwork directly out through a dedicated exterior roof cap with an integrated damper, install attic cardboard rafter baffles, and seal bypass penetrations.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </article>

      <article class="surface-bento calc-strategy-card">
        <header class="text-center">
          <div class="text-overline text-accent-alpine">Foundation &amp; Subfloor</div>
          <h3 class="text-widget-title">Damp Crawl Space &amp; Musty Earth Odor</h3>
        </header>

        <div class="section-grid-static grid-cols-2">
          <div class="calc-field-group">
            <span class="calc-field-label">Primary Root Cause</span>
            <p class="text-body">Bare earth soil floor evaporating hundreds of gallons of ground moisture annually directly into floor joists, or torn and bunched plastic sheeting.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="calc-field-group">
            <span class="calc-field-label">Permanent Mechanical Fix</span>
            <p class="text-body">Install a continuous, 100 percent coverage 6-mil black polyethylene vapor barrier with 12-inch overlapping seams staked to foundation stem walls.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </article>

      <article class="surface-bento calc-strategy-card">
        <header class="text-center">
          <div class="text-overline text-accent-alpine">Living Areas</div>
          <h3 class="text-widget-title">Window Glass Condensation &amp; Tile Mildew</h3>
        </header>

        <div class="section-grid-static grid-cols-2">
          <div class="calc-field-group">
            <span class="calc-field-label">Primary Root Cause</span>
            <p class="text-body">Trapped indoor relative humidity exceeding 60 percent during winter months due to lack of mechanical exhaust air exchange.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="calc-field-group">
            <span class="calc-field-label">Permanent Mechanical Fix</span>
            <p class="text-body">Upgrade to quiet 80+ CFM continuous or timer-controlled exhaust fans, run fans 20 minutes post-shower, and open window frame trickle vents.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </article>

    </div>
  </section>

